          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-73d98923c166b6da\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Natasha directly avows that she will continue to love Denisov, primarily presenting enduring affectionate regard as true. The future-oriented “always” also has a moderate promise-like or reassuring force, but it does not accept his proposal or undertake a marital relationship. Her emotional and hesitant delivery does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The future construction can be heard either as a prediction of lasting affection or as a limited commitment to continue caring for him; this supports moderate rather than maximal P.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Natasha directly addresses Denisov while refusing his marriage proposal and tells him that she will always love him nonetheless.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration softens a compassionate but definite rejection. Natasha has just told her mother that she is not in love with Denisov, yet finds him dear and pities him; accordingly, “love” here denotes continuing personal affection rather than reciprocal romantic love or acceptance of marriage. The utterance reassures Denisov and preserves their bond while explicitly withholding the relationship he requested.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"«но не надо... это... а так я вас всегда буду любить» (“but it must not be… this… but I shall always love you all the same”)\",\"supports\":\"The contrast directly couples rejection of the proposal with an avowal of enduring affection, strongly supporting T and distinguishing the utterance from marital acceptance.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Before speaking to Denisov, Natasha says: «я не влюблена в него, должно быть не влюблена в него» (“I am not in love with him; I must not be in love with him”).\",\"supports\":\"Shows that the later love declaration concerns non-conjugal affection rather than reciprocal romantic love, without negating its truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Natasha repeatedly says she feels sorry for Denisov and calls him dear or wonderful; after the refusal she kisses his head and later sobs on seeing his distress.\",\"supports\":\"Establishes the compassionate, consoling context. The emotion explains intensity but provides no positive evidence that the words themselves are a reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The highlighted form is affirmative and future-oriented: «я вас всегда буду любить».\",\"supports\":\"The future construction and «всегда» (“always”) support some promise-like enduring commitment in addition to the avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the avowal of enduring affection, while moderate P captures its limited reassurance or promise-like orientation. Emotional delivery remains contextual, and no important core force falls outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
